// levels.ts
const LEVEL_STARTS = [0, 100, 300, 600, 1000]; // Level 1..5

export function levelFromExp(exp: number): number {
  let level = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i < LEVEL_STARTS.length; i++) {
    if (exp > LEVEL_STARTS[i]) level = i + 1;
  }
  return level;
}

The only test of the profile page is an end-to-end test that signs up a new learner (0 EXP) and checks that the page shows "Level 1".

Statement: "A unit test that calls levelFromExp(1000) and expects 5 would have caught this bug, without a database, a server or a browser."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The bug is a comparison inside one function: exp > 1000 is false for exactly 1000, so the loop never reaches Level 5. levelFromExp takes a number and returns a number, so a unit test with the value from the bug report (1000, and 999 next to it) fails on this code in milliseconds and keeps failing until the comparison is >=. The end-to-end test could never see it: a new learner has 0 EXP, far from any boundary. The misconception is that a bug noticed on a page needs a test that goes through the page; where the bug is noticed and where it lives are different places.', 1, 5
