SELECT user_id, course_id, count(*) FROM enrollments GROUP BY 1, 2 HAVING count(*) > 1;
 user_id   | course_id  | count
 u_88213   | sql-basics |     2

The service:

export async function enrollLearner(repo: EnrollmentRepo, userId: string, courseId: string) {
  if (await repo.exists(userId, courseId)) return;   // SELECT 1 FROM enrollments WHERE ...
  await repo.insert(userId, courseId);              // INSERT INTO enrollments ...
}

The enrollments table has no unique constraint on (user_id, course_id). The existing test uses an in-memory fake:

it("does not enroll twice", async () => {
  const repo = new InMemoryEnrollmentRepo();
  await enrollLearner(repo, "u1", "sql-basics");
  await enrollLearner(repo, "u1", "sql-basics");
  expect(await repo.count("u1", "sql-basics")).toBe(1);
}); // passes

Statement: "Another unit test with the in-memory fake repository, sending the two enroll calls at the same time with Promise.all, is the cheapest test that reliably catches this bug."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Two requests can both run the SELECT before either INSERT, and only the database can stop the second insert: the missing piece is a unique constraint on (user_id, course_id) with INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING. The fake runs no SQL and has no constraint, so whether it lets the race through depends on how the fake happens to be written, not on the real table; and after the fix in the database, the fake would still behave the same. The cheapest test that can see this bug is an integration test against PostgreSQL that enrolls the same learner twice, concurrently, and counts the rows. The misconception is that a unit test is always the cheapest choice; it is only cheapest among tests that can actually see the bug.', 2, 5
