The progress code:

function progressPercent(requiredIds: number[], completions: { lessonId: number }[]) {
  const done = completions.filter((c) => requiredIds.includes(c.lessonId)).length;
  return Math.floor((done * 100) / requiredIds.length);
}

Statement: "A unit test that calls progressPercent([1, 2, 3, 4], [{ lessonId: 1 }, { lessonId: 2 }, { lessonId: 3 }, { lessonId: 3 }]) and expects 75 fails on this code."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. filter keeps all four events, because lesson 3 appears twice, so done is 4 and the function returns 100. The test expects 75, so it fails, which is exactly what you want a test for repeated requests to do. The fix is to count distinct lesson ids (a Set) instead of events. Note that the test needs no server, no database and no double click: two identical events in an array reproduce the bug in milliseconds. The misconception is that duplicate requests are a concurrency problem that only an end-to-end test can show; here the duplicate is just data.', 2, 5
