  const today = now.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
  const quiz = { id: "ts-quiz-3", deadline: `${today}T23:59`, utcOffsetMinutes: 420, graceMinutes: 15 };
  expect(submissionStatus(quiz, { now: () => now })).toBe("on-time");
});

Statement: "This test can still fail when the nightly CI run starts at 01:30 Vietnam time."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The function no longer reads the real clock, but the test does: it builds both now and the deadline from new Date(). At 01:30 on 28 October in Vietnam the instant is 2026-10-27T18:30Z, so today is "2026-10-27" and the deadline is 27 October 23:59 in UTC+7 (16:59Z), more than the 15-minute grace in the past: the function correctly answers closed. The misconception is that injecting a clock makes a test deterministic by itself. It only helps when the test passes a fixed instant, for example { now: () => new Date("2026-10-31T16:59:00Z") } with a fixed deadline.', 2, 5
