WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ttd_221'), 'A teammate''s in-memory fake and a new service method:

class FakeEnrollmentRepository implements EnrollmentRepository {
  private rows = new Map<string, Enrollment>();
  async findById(id: string) { return this.rows.get(id) ?? null; }
  // create, findByUser, update, delete ...
}

async complete(id: string) {            // in EnrollmentService
  const e = await this.repo.findById(id);
  if (!e) return null;
  e.progress = 100;
  e.status = "completed";
  return e;
}

The test passes with the fake:

await service.complete("e1");
expect((await repo.findById("e1"))?.status).toBe("completed");

Statement: "With PostgresEnrollmentRepository the same assertion would fail, because complete() never writes the change back with update()."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The fake''s findById returns the very object stored in its Map, so changing e also changes the fake''s stored row and the next read sees status completed. PostgreSQL returns a new object built from the row on every read; the service changed only its own copy and never called repo.update(id, { progress: 100, status: "completed" }), so the database still holds active. The misconception is that any in-memory fake behaves like the database. A fake must return copies (a contract test can check it: change a returned object and read it back), otherwise it hides exactly this kind of missing write.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ttd_221') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
