Statement: "Commits 4e1c9a2 and 9d03b7e each failed and then passed with exactly the same code, so the result depends on something that changes from run to run - such as the time a test runs at, the order the tests run in, or state left behind by another test - and not on the code alone."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. A re-run uses the same commit, the same dependencies and the same tests, yet the result flips, so the outcome depends on an input that is not in the code: the clock, the order of the tests, state shared between tests, randomness, or the network. The history also argues against the usual first suspect, a slow CI machine: failed runs take the same time as passing ones (median 2m 40s against 2m 41s). The misconception is to treat "passed on re-run" as "nothing is wrong"; it is the definition of a flaky test, and each failure is a real outcome that happens with some probability.', 1, 5
