-  if (today > coupon.expiresOn) return price;
+  if (today >= coupon.expiresOn) return price;

A reviewer proposes one more test:
test("coupon used on its expiry day", () =>
  expect(applyCoupon(199000, { kind: "fixed", value: 20000, expiresOn: "2026-10-26" }, "2026-10-26")).toBe(179000));

Statement: "The new test passes on applyCoupon and fails on this mutant, so it kills the mutant: on the mutant it returns 199000."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. On the real code today (2026-10-26) is not after expiresOn (2026-10-26), so the coupon applies and the result is 199,000 - 20,000 = 179,000: the test passes. On the mutant, today >= expiresOn is true on the expiry day, so it returns the full 199,000 and the test fails: the mutant is killed. The existing "expired coupon" test could not do this, because 2026-10-26 is after 2026-10-01 for both > and >=. A mutant on a comparison is killed by a case exactly on the boundary, which is the same rule as in lesson 1.2. The misconception is that one test for "expired" and one for "valid" cover the expiry rule; the day itself is the case that matters.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ttd_331') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
