$ npx vitest run --coverage
File       | % Stmts | % Branch | % Funcs | % Lines
coupon.ts  |   100   |   100    |   100   |   100

$ npx stryker run
File       | % score | # killed | # timeout | # survived | # no cov | # errors
coupon.ts  |  57.14  |    4     |     0     |     3      |    0     |    0

Statement: "The two reports do not contradict each other: coverage says every line of coupon.ts ran during the tests, and the mutation score says 3 of the 7 changes made to those lines went unnoticed by the tests."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Coverage records which code was executed. Mutation testing changes the code (a mutant), runs the tests again and checks whether at least one test fails. A line can run in every test while no assertion depends on what it computes, so 100% coverage and 3 surviving mutants are perfectly compatible: "no cov 0" even confirms every mutant sat on covered code. The score here is killed / (killed + survived + no coverage) = 4 / 7. The misconception is that coverage measures how well the code is tested; it measures how much of it was run.', 1, 5
