    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ttd_341'), 'A pull request changes the coupon rule; its tests were written by an AI assistant.

$ git diff --name-only origin/main
src/checkout/coupon.ts
src/checkout/coupon.test.ts

$ npx stryker run --mutate src/checkout/coupon.ts
coupon.ts  % score 28.57  # killed 2  # survived 5  # no cov 0
Line coverage of coupon.ts: 100%

Statement: "Running Stryker only on the changed file is a reasonable check for this pull request, and the 5 surviving mutants are a concrete list of behaviours that the AI-written tests do not check."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Mutation testing is slow because it runs the tests once per mutant, so limiting it to the files a pull request changes (here with --mutate, or with an incremental mode that reuses earlier results) keeps it fast enough for review, and a full run can be scheduled nightly. Each survivor names a line and a change that no test noticed - for example a boundary or a rounding rule - which is exactly what a reviewer needs to ask for better tests, whoever wrote them. Check the options of your Stryker version in its documentation. The misconception is that tests generated together with the code check the code; here they run every line and kill 2 of 7 mutants.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ttd_341') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
